:::
:::


The missing results come from 15 tract-period observations: seven in
2006–2010 and eight in 2013–2017. In every case, the estimated population for
whom poverty status was determined (`totalE`) is zero, so a poverty rate cannot
be calculated. Seven tracts have this problem in both periods, while Census
Tract 9806 has a usable estimate in 2006–2010 but not in 2013–2017. I do not
treat these missing rates as zero. I exclude them because zero means there is
no estimated population in the poverty universe, not that the poverty rate is
zero.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
poverty_rates_clean <- poverty_rates %>%
  filter(
    !is.na(poverty_rate),
    !is.na(poverty_rate_moe)
  )
```
:::


### Reliability

I calculate the coefficient of variation using the poverty-rate margin of
error. Following Jurjevich et al. (2018), estimates with a CV below 12 percent
are classified as reliable; estimates from 12 to 40 percent are somewhat
reliable; and estimates above 40 percent are unreliable.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
poverty_rates_clean <- poverty_rates_clean %>%
  mutate(
    cv = (poverty_rate_moe / 1.645) / poverty_rate * 100,
    reliability = case_when(
      cv < 12 ~ "Reliable",
      cv <= 40 ~ "Somewhat reliable",
      TRUE ~ "Unreliable"
    )
  )
```
:::



::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
poverty_rates_clean %>%
  count(period, reliability)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
# A tibble: 6 × 3
  period    reliability           n
  <chr>     <chr>             <int>
1 2006-2010 Reliable             11
2 2006-2010 Somewhat reliable   321
3 2006-2010 Unreliable           45
4 2013-2017 Reliable             22
5 2013-2017 Somewhat reliable   332
6 2013-2017 Unreliable           22
```


:::
:::


The table reports the reliability of every usable tract estimate in the
2013–2017 ACS period. The color-coded flags encourage cautious interpretation
rather than automatically excluding valid ACS observations.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
poverty_rates_clean %>%
  filter(period == "2013-2017") %>%
  mutate(
    reliability_symbol = case_when(
      cv < 12  ~ "🟢",
      cv <= 40 ~ "🟡",
      TRUE     ~ "🔴"
    ),
    tract = str_remove(NAME, ", Philadelphia County, Pennsylvania"),
    rate = poverty_rate,
    rate_moe = poverty_rate_moe
  ) %>%
  arrange(desc(cv)) %>%
  select(reliability_symbol, tract, rate, rate_moe, cv, reliability) %>%
  gt() %>%
  tab_header(
    title = "Reliability of Philadelphia tract poverty-rate estimates",
    subtitle = "2013-2017 ACS five-year estimates"
  ) %>%
  cols_label(
    reliability_symbol = "",
    tract = "Census tract",
    rate = "Poverty rate",
    rate_moe = "MOE",
    cv = "CV (%)",
    reliability = "Reliability"
  ) %>%
  fmt_percent(columns = rate, decimals = 1) %>%
  fmt_percent(columns = rate_moe, decimals = 1) %>%
  fmt_number(columns = cv, decimals = 1) %>%
  cols_align(align = "center", columns = reliability_symbol) %>%
  tab_source_note(
    "Source: U.S. Census Bureau ACS five-year estimates via tidycensus. Jurjevich et al. (2018) thresholds: CV < 12% reliable; 12-40% somewhat reliable; > 40% unreliable."
  )%>%
  tab_options(
    # keep the sheet into a box, so that it will be easier to read.
    container.height = px(500),
    container.overflow.y = TRUE,
    container.width = pct(100),
    container.overflow.x = TRUE
  )
```

